Z||000000||BroCar 87||01-29-2001||10:29 PM||||Alright people, I want the wheel combo like the mustangs have around here. Little ones in front, bigger ones in back. I am looking through my summit and jegs catalogs, and don't know which rims to buy. I know nothing about how to decide which is the best or what will fit. For the front, What kind of rear spacing (in inches) is needed? And for the back, How wide should/could I go?<P>Aren't the backspacing of our current rims around 35mm.? On a ruler, thats not even 1-1/2 inches. Do I get more backspacing or less backspacing???    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><BR>Going crazy trying to figure these rims out!<BR>Thanks for any help or info!<BR>BroCar 87||||reg||6
Z||000001||Standard||01-30-2001||01:33 AM||||I don't think you can go much wider than 8 or 9 inches in back, unless you switch to coil-overs.  And I'm pretty sure that the wider the wheel is, the more offset you need.  If you go to a wider wheel, but keep the same offset, it will stick out from the sides of the car.  A larger (+)offset moves the wheels in towards the center of the car.  Well... it moves the mounting surface out from the center of the wheel, which tucks more of the rim under the car.  <P>The amount of offset is measured from the centerline of the wheel to the mounting surface.  A + offset moves the mounting suface towards the outside of the rim, a - off set does the opposite.  <P>Not sure what the offset of the stock rims is... and I could be completly bass ackwards on all of this, or just plain wrong  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  giving me a headache just trying to sort it all out... ||||reg||
Z||000002||Oreif||01-30-2001||02:13 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BroCar 87:<BR><B>Alright people, I want the wheel combo like the mustangs have around here. Little ones in front, bigger ones in back. BroCar 87</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>WHY? Little tires in front with wide tires in back is great for straight line, But a Fiero was designed for corners.<P>Like the AD says:<BR>"Happiness isn't around the corner.......<BR>Happiness IS the corner"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||BroCar 87||01-30-2001||11:39 AM||||HAHA sweeet..."Happiness IS the Corner" <BR>Well I know once I get my V8 project complete, I will be spending alot of time at the track. I just want to get the best times I can while I am there. I would like to have the option to swap my rims and tires from street to strip. <P>Plus I LOVE the look, people see the little racing tires up front (bout 4 inches wide) and they KNOW you mean business!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Thanks, anyone else?? <BR>BroCar 87||||reg||6
